course: reflection
lesson: 4
title: Letting a friend down: broken promises and spilled secrets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Letting someone down leaves a special kind of sting: you replay the moment, picture their face, and call yourself names in the dark.
explain: Sam promised his best mate Jake he'd come to his birthday, then cooler kids asked him to the cinema and he went; Jake's mum texted, "Jake waited at the door for an hour," and that night Sam couldn't eat, thinking, "I'm a proper snake. He waited a whole hour. For ME."
explain: His first text, "soz", made it worse; what helped was saying sorry face to face with no excuses and then doing something just the two of them, because a sorry has to come with changed actions, not only words.
explain: The guilt is not there to punish you forever: it is your conscience pointing at what matters, like real friends, kept promises and kept secrets, and it usually fades into a lesson once you have tried to put things right.
explain: Trust breaks fast and grows back slowly, so fixing it takes patience; and one forgotten job or one dropped baton does not make you useless.
fact: Guilt after letting someone down is the conscience pointing at what matters; it usually fades into a lesson once you try to put things right.
fact: A real apology names what you did, gives no excuses and comes with changed actions; a quick "soz" text often makes it worse.
fact: Trust breaks fast and grows back slowly; secrets can feel powerful to share, but trust is worth more.
fact: Forgetting a job is human; owning up to a teacher or grown-up quickly, and then helping fix the problem, is what responsibility means.
fact: One mistake rarely decides a team result on its own; everyone on a team has good and bad moments, and even top athletes drop the baton.
fact: Younger brothers and sisters notice everything big siblings do; the people who look up to you get hurt most when you forget them.
fact: A bystander is someone who watches but does not help; fear freezes many people the first time, and even a small "that's not nice" or telling a teacher counts.
fact: Being trusted with a job by a grown-up, like walking a dog, is a big deal; breaking that trust can still be fixed by going back, apologising and being reliable.
fact: Lying to parents about where you are scares them, because they need to know you are safe; the lie often hurts more than the place you went.
fact: Forgetting a friend's birthday does not mean you don't care; saying sorry fast and doing something special fixes most of it ("belated" means late).
fact: After a mistake people often feel that "everyone hates me", but most people forget quickly.
fact: Calling yourself "useless" or "a terrible friend" after one mistake is unfair to yourself; you did a bad thing, you are not a bad person.
exercise: Sam went to the cinema instead of Jake's party, and Jake waited at the door for an hour. What did Sam learn? | Real friends are worth more than looking cool | Cool kids are always the better friends to choose | Birthday parties are not important to anyone | It is fine to break a promise if you get a better offer
exercise: Sam first texted Jake "soz". Why did that make it worse? | It was a tiny sorry for a big hurt, with no change | Texting a sorry is against the rules between proper friends | Jake does not know what the word "soz" means | Saying sorry always makes everything worse
exercise: Grace told the class her friend's secret to make two other girls like her. What is true about trust? | It breaks fast and grows back slowly | It breaks slowly and grows back in a day | It can never be broken by telling a secret | It grows back as soon as you say "it's not that deep"
exercise: What helped Grace start to fix things with her friend? | A real sorry and standing up for her friend when others teased | Saying "it's not that deep" again and again until her friend calmed down | Telling a different secret to make things even | Waiting for her friend to say sorry first
exercise: Oliver forgot to feed the class tadpoles over half-term and half died. He thinks, "I'm useless." What is true? | He forgot one job, which is human; owning up is the right thing | He really is useless and should never be given any job in class again | Tadpoles don't need food, so it wasn't really his fault | He should hide in the toilets until everyone forgets
exercise: What does "owning it" mean? | Taking responsibility for what you did | Buying the thing you broke so that it belongs to you | Saying that it was really somebody else's idea | Hiding the problem quietly until it goes away
exercise: Ava dropped the baton and her relay team came last. A girl said, "Thanks a lot, Ava." What is true? | One mistake rarely decides a race alone; even Olympic runners drop it | Ava lost the race on her own, and the other three runners were all perfect | Ava should never run in a team again after this | Dropping a baton only happens to people who don't practise
exercise: Leo forgot his little brother's scary school show; his brother looked for him and cried. What fixed it best? | Going to the next show and cheering loudest from the front row | Buying him a big bag of sweets after the show so he would stop crying | Telling him the show didn't really matter anyway | Promising to come without changing anything
exercise: Lucy asked Ruby for help when kids teased her, but Ruby froze. What is true? | Fear freezes many; next time a small "that's not nice" counts | Ruby is a coward forever and can never change it, however hard she tries | Bystanders are never part of the problem at all | Ruby should avoid Lucy so she doesn't feel bad
exercise: What is a bystander? | Someone who watches something bad happen but doesn't help | Someone who stands at the same bus stop every single morning | Someone who starts the teasing in a group and gets others to join | Someone who always reports any bullying to a teacher straight away
exercise: Kofi skipped walking his ill neighbour's dog for three days to play a video game. What helped him? | Going back, saying sorry and walking the dog every day after | Avoiding her front door for weeks until she had forgotten all about it | Telling her the dog didn't need walking anyway | Paying another kid to walk the dog instead
exercise: Holly said she was at Emma's but was at the park with older kids, and Mum cried when she found out. Why did Mum cry? | She was scared because she didn't know where Holly was | She hates parks and doesn't want Holly to have fun | She is angry that Holly has friends who are older than her | She cries every time anyone goes out of the house
exercise: Mateo forgot his best friend's birthday. What fixed most of it? | Saying sorry fast and giving a funny handmade card | Pretending that he had remembered the birthday all along | Ignoring the birthday completely until it came round next year | Waiting quietly for his friend to bring it up first
exercise: Amira left the team poster on the bus, and she thinks, "Everyone hates me now." What usually happens? | Most people forget quickly, and helping next time rebuilds trust | Everyone hates her now and will keep on hating her for the rest of primary school | She should skip school until the next project | Teams never forgive one mistake
exercise: Which is true about the guilt after letting someone down? | It points at what matters and usually fades once you try to fix it | It is a punishment you deserve, and it should last for your whole life | It means you are a bad person who can't change | It goes away best if you never say sorry
test: Your mate from London goes, "bruv, you bailed on his party for the cinema? that's bare snakey." ("Bailed" means didn't turn up; "bare" means very; a "snake" is a friend who betrays you.) What should Sam do now? | Say sorry face to face without excuses and make time for Jake | Send a quick "soz" and hope it blows over | Tell Jake the party would have been boring anyway | Stay away from Jake until he forgets the whole thing
test: A girl says to Grace, "it's not that deep, it's only a secret." Why is she wrong? | Telling a private secret breaks trust that grows back slowly | She is right, secrets never matter to anyone | She is right, because the friend would have told everyone the secret anyway | She is wrong only because the secret was boring
test: In a cartoon, a boy breaks a promise, says "sorry!" once, and his friend instantly trusts him completely again. What's unrealistic? | Trust usually grows back slowly, with changed actions over time | Nothing; one "sorry" always brings trust straight back | Real friends never forgive anything at all | In real life you don't need to say sorry at all
test: Coach, Scouse accent: "Ava, la, it happens to the best, even the Olympians." ("La" is like "mate".) What is the coach teaching her? | One dropped baton doesn't make her a bad runner | She should give up the relay team for good | Olympians drop batons because they don't practise | Mistakes only matter at the Olympics
test: What would happen if Oliver hid the dead tadpoles and said nothing? | He'd likely feel worse, and nobody could help fix the problem | The tadpoles would come back to life on their own | Everyone would trust him more with jobs | The teacher would never need to know anything, and the tadpoles would be fine
test: Leo's big cousin, a Gen Z gamer, says, "the little guy lowkey idolises you, fr." ("Idolises" means looks up to a lot; "fr" means for real.) Why does forgetting his show hurt so much? | People who look up to you get hurt most when you forget them | Little kids never notice what their older siblings do | Shows are boring, so his brother was only pretending | It doesn't hurt, because small kids forget everything
test: Ruby thinks: "I stood there like a lemon. I'm a coward forever." ("Stood there like a lemon" means did nothing.) What's a fairer thought? | Fear froze me this time, but next time I can be braver | I am a coward, so I should never try to help anyone | Bystanders are never responsible for anything | Lucy should have stood up for herself instead
test: Kofi's neighbour said, "I thought I could count on you." What does "reliable" mean? | Someone you can trust to do what they promised | Someone who always says yes to every single request | Someone who never ever makes a mistake of any kind | Someone who pays other people to do their jobs for them
test: Holly's mum, from Leeds: "I were going spare, love!" ("Going spare" means frantic with worry.) What helped them afterwards? | Saying sorry and agreeing to text where she goes | Arguing about the punishment until Mum finally gave in | Promising to lie better and get home earlier next time | Never going out of the house again, even with friends
test: A kid on the bus says, "if you forget your best mate's birthday, you're done, friendship over." Is that true? | No; a quick sorry and something special fixes most of it | Yes; forgetting once always ends a friendship forever | Yes, unless you buy a really expensive present | No, because birthdays don't matter to anyone
test: A boy says to Amira, sarcastic: "Cheers, Amira, we're all getting a C." What does "cheers" mean here? | Thanks for nothing; he is blaming her | Thank you very much; he is truly grateful to her | Well done; he is proud of the whole team | Goodbye; he is leaving the group right now
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an adequate apology? | It acknowledges the wrong without excuse and is accompanied by altered conduct | It consists solely of a brief written message despatched electronically | It is effective only where the injured party apologises first | It obliges the injured party to restore complete trust immediately
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of trust, as established in the lesson. | It is lost rapidly and restored only gradually | It is restored immediately upon any expression of regret | It cannot be diminished by the disclosure of confidences | It is unaffected by the conduct of the parties
exam: It is given that a pupil has neglected an assigned duty. Determine the course of action most consistent with responsibility. | Prompt disclosure to the responsible adult and aid in the remedy | Concealment of the omission pending its discovery by others | Attribution of the omission to another pupil | Permanent refusal of all future duties
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a person who witnesses unkindness but does not intervene. | Such a person is termed a bystander, and fear commonly induces such inaction | Such a person is invariably the instigator of the unkindness | Such a person bears no relation whatsoever to the incident | Such a person is incapable of acting differently on any future occasion
